import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
const title = 'Cargo insurance for peptide shipments';
const description = 'Who insures a peptide shipment under each Incoterm, what cargo clauses cover, why temperature damage is usually excluded, and how a claim is documented.';
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Made in China Peptide`} description={description} canonical="/knowledge/peptide-cargo-insurance/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Knowledge',href:'/knowledge/'},{label:'Cargo insurance'}]} />
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Freight is the part of a peptide purchase where the buyer has the least control and the most exposure. A shipment can be lost, damaged, delayed, frozen, cooked or held — and the question of who bears the loss is decided by two documents most buyers read after something goes wrong: the Incoterm and the insurance clause. This is a practical map, not insurance or legal advice.</p>

    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-shipping-customs-framework/">shipping &amp; customs pillar</a> — the end-to-end export framework.</p>
    <h2>Who is exposed under each Incoterm</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Risk passes</th><th>Who usually insures</th><th>Practical note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>EXW</td><td>At the seller's premises</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Buyer carries everything from the dock; insurance should start before the first carrier touches the goods</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FCA</td><td>When handed to the carrier nominated by the buyer</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Cleaner than EXW for export clearance; cover from the handover point</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FOB</td><td>When goods are on board the vessel</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Common for sea freight; the buyer's cover should begin at the port of loading</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CIF</td><td>When goods are on board</td><td>Seller, for the buyer's benefit</td><td>Minimum cover is usually clause C at 110% of invoice value; that is not all-risk</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CIP</td><td>When handed to the first carrier</td><td>Seller, for the buyer's benefit</td><td>Requires clause A cover under the current rules; broader than CIF</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DAP / DDP</td><td>At destination, ready for unloading (DAP) or duty paid (DDP)</td><td>Seller</td><td>Seller bears transit risk; the buyer should still confirm the cover and the temperature rider</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The table describes the default allocation. The contract can agree something different, and the insurance clause should be read as carefully as the price. Under CIF, “insurance arranged” is not a synonym for “fully covered”: the default minimum is narrower than most buyers assume.</p>

    <h2>The three cargo clauses</h2>
    <p>Marine cargo policies are usually written on institute cargo clauses, which come in three levels:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Clause A — all risks</strong> (subject to the exclusions in the policy). The broadest cover, and the appropriate default for high-value or fragile cargo.</li>
      <li><strong>Clause B</strong> — named perils plus a few additional events such as earthquake, volcanic eruption, washing overboard and water damage from certain causes.</li>
      <li><strong>Clause C</strong> — a narrower named-perils list, typically major casualties: fire, explosion, stranding, collision, discharge at a port of distress. It does not cover ordinary handling damage or theft.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Clause C is the usual CIF minimum, which is why a CIF shipment can be “insured” and still leave a damaged carton uncompensated. For a peptide shipment, the value per kilogram is high and the packaging is often the weak point, so clause A is the more realistic choice.</p>

    <h2>What is usually excluded — and why temperature matters</h2>
    <p>Standard cargo policies exclude loss caused by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inherent vice</strong> — the tendency of the goods to deteriorate on their own, including instability that is not caused by an external event.</li>
      <li><strong>Insufficient or unsuitable packing</strong> — if the shipper's packaging was not adequate for the journey, the insurer can decline.</li>
      <li><strong>Delay</strong> — consequential loss from late arrival is generally not covered, even when the delay is the carrier's fault.</li>
      <li><strong>Ordinary leakage, wear and tear, and handling damage</strong> under the narrower clauses.</li>
      <li><strong>Temperature</strong> — a standard policy does not cover damage from heat or cold unless a specific temperature clause or rider is added.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The temperature exclusion is the one that catches peptide buyers. A lyophilised cake that sat on a tarmac in summer, or a solution that froze in an unheated hold, may arrive looking normal and fail later. To have a claim, the policy needs a temperature clause that defines the covered range, the evidence required and whether the cover is for physical damage or for deterioration. A data logger recording the journey is the difference between an argument and a claim.</p>

    <h2>Insuring the right value</h2>
    <p>Sum insured should reflect the replacement cost of the goods, not only the invoice line. Freight, insurance and duty can be a meaningful fraction of the landed cost, and a total loss claim that reimburses only the ex-works price leaves the buyer short. CIF conventions use invoice value plus 10%, but that is a floor, not a considered sum for a scarce or long-lead item.</p>
    <p>Also check the deductible. A policy with a low premium and a high deductible can be effectively useless for a small parcel; the deductible should be small relative to the shipment value.</p>

    <h2>Arranging cover before the goods move</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Decide the Incoterm first.</strong> It determines who buys the insurance and when risk passes.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the clause and the temperature rider in writing.</strong> “All risk” on a certificate is not enough if the policy excludes temperature.</li>
      <li><strong>Insure from the handover point, not from arrival.</strong> Cover that starts at the destination port leaves the main journey uninsured.</li>
      <li><strong>Declare the cargo accurately.</strong> “Research chemicals” may be acceptable; a misdescription that hides the nature of the goods can void a claim.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep the commercial invoice, packing list and carrier documents consistent</strong> with what was declared to the insurer.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>If something goes wrong</h2>
    <p>Claims are decided on documents and on timing. A workable sequence:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Notify the insurer or broker promptly</strong>, within the notice period in the policy. Late notice is a common reason for denial.</li>
      <li><strong>Preserve the packaging and the goods.</strong> Do not discard a damaged carton or repack the contents before an inspection; the insurer may want a survey.</li>
      <li><strong>Record the condition on delivery.</strong> Note damage on the carrier's delivery receipt, photograph the carton, the inner packaging and the vials, and keep the temperature logger data.</li>
      <li><strong>Collect the claim pack:</strong> invoice, packing list, bill of lading or air waybill, insurance certificate, survey report, photographs, temperature record and a statement of the loss.</li>
      <li><strong>Quantify the loss</strong> as repair, replacement or total loss, with evidence of the replacement cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Preserve subrogation rights.</strong> Do not sign a full release in favour of the carrier without the insurer's agreement.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a temperature excursion without visible damage, the claim is harder: the evidence is the logger data plus an analytical result showing deterioration. That is why arrival testing and a retained sample matter; the analytical record is what turns “it may have been warm” into a documented loss. The arrival checks are covered in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-cold-chain-logistics/">cold chain and logistics</a>.</p>

    <h2>What to ask before the first shipment</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Which Incoterm applies, and where exactly does risk pass?</li>
      <li>Who arranges insurance, and what clause level is specified?</li>
      <li>Is temperature damage covered, over what range, and what evidence is required?</li>
      <li>Is the sum insured the invoice value or the landed replacement cost?</li>
      <li>What is the deductible, and what is the notice period for a claim?</li>
      <li>Does the policy cover the full door-to-door journey, including any customs hold?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The answers belong in the contract or the order confirmation, not in an email after the loss. For the wider allocation of risk in the purchase, see <a href="/knowledge/peptide-procurement-contract-terms/">procurement contract terms</a>; for the documents that travel with the goods, see <a href="/knowledge/shipping-peptides-customs-guide/">shipping and customs</a>.</p>

    <div class="ruo">For laboratory research use only. This article describes commercial risk allocation; it is not insurance or legal advice, and it makes no claim about the coverage of any specific policy. It does not support administration to humans or animals.</div>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Cargo insurance is the cheapest risk transfer in this trade and the one most often skipped. If the policy excludes temperature damage, you have insured the box, not the product.</p>

